verdict: sqrt(n) never wins and rule 2's power-law family wins 3% of resamples; the contest is affine (a>0) versus pure linear at 52% vs 46%. Their n=400 predictions overlap, so one more doubling cannot split them; the discriminating point is small n (the intercept), not large n. An n=400 run would still separate sqrt from linear.
